WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.880
How do you calculate scheduling

2
00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:04.380
performance? Begin with a timeline,

3
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.920
not a memorized formula with

4
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.300
unexplained letters. We will track

5
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.620
arrival, first execution, completion, processor

6
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.480
work and blocked time separately,

7
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.960
because different metrics answer different

8
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:18.440
questions.

9
00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.180
Our authored process arrives at

10
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.120
time zero and waits ready

11
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.240
until time two. It runs

12
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.980
from two to four, waits

13
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.160
in a device queue from

14
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.600
four to five, and receives

15
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.220
device service from five to

16
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:32.900
eight. It then waits ready

17
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.060
until nine, runs until eleven,

18
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:35.760
and finishes.

19
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.200
Response time measures the delay

20
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.040
to the first execution. First

21
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.320
run is two and arrival

22
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.180
is zero, so response is

23
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.800
two minus zero, equal to

24
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.740
two. Later preemptions or device

25
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.340
operations do not reset that

26
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:49.520
first response measurement.

27
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.380
Turnaround measures the entire time

28
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.020
from arrival to completion. Completion

29
00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.460
is eleven and arrival is

30
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.880
zero, giving eleven units. That

31
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.820
interval includes processor execution, ready-queue

32
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.500
waiting, device-queue waiting and actual

33
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.140
device service in this example.

34
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.380
Total processor execution is two

35
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.220
plus two, equal to four

36
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.180
units. Ready-queue waiting is the

37
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:14.740
initial two units plus the

38
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:15.960
unit from eight to nine,

39
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.740
giving three. The remaining blocked

40
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.200
interval from four to eight

41
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.740
has one unit queued for

42
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.220
the device and three units

43
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:23.820
of service.

44
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.100
Under the ready-only definition, subtract

45
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.040
total processor work and all

46
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.460
blocked time from turnaround. Eleven

47
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.120
minus four minus four gives

48
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.380
three units waiting for the

49
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.920
processor. That calculation agrees with

50
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.340
adding the two ready intervals

51
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:38.880
directly.

52
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.380
The photographed course exercise includes

53
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:43.740
time in the ready queue

54
00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.360
and time queued for a

55
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.960
device, while excluding actual device

56
00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.520
service. Under that convention, eleven

57
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.360
minus four minus three gives

58
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.500
four. State which waiting definition

59
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.200
you are using before calculating,

60
00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.240
because the two definitions count

61
00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.260
different intervals.

62
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.540
Throughput counts completed processes per

63
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.320
unit of time, not the

64
00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.220
number currently waiting. If two

65
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.360
processes complete across ten time

66
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.740
units, throughput is two divided

67
00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.920
by ten. Do not confuse

68
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.520
this system-wide rate with the

69
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:16.880
turnaround of one process.

70
00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.160
For two cores observed over

71
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.380
ten time units, suppose the

72
00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:23.200
useful busy times total fifteen

73
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.260
core-time units. Utilization is fifteen

74
00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.240
divided by two times ten,

75
00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.300
or seventy-five percent. Dividing by

76
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.620
ten alone would incorrectly exceed

77
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.060
one hundred percent for a

78
00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.140
multicore aggregate.

79
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.080
Things to take note of.

80
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.720
Calculate each process metric before

81
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.420
averaging, keep units consistent, and

82
00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.820
distinguish first execution from completion.

83
00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.700
If the question has one

84
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.580
processor burst and no blocking,

85
00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.300
waiting simplifies to completion minus

86
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.740
arrival minus burst; do not

87
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.800
carry that shortcut unchanged into

88
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:57.940
an input-output trace.
